   "transcript": "Interviewer: Do you eat to live or live to eat? Either way our subject today is healthy eating and again, thank you very much. We’ve had many enquiries from around the world asking about what constitutes a healthy diet, and how to get the best out of the food that’s available locally, especially perhaps for growing children. And here with me today to answer your emails, your letters and your live phone calls is Catherine Geissler. She’s professor of Nutrition at King’s College in London. Professor Geissler, what’s more important, what we eat or how much we eat?\nProf Catherine Geissler: Both of these are important. In many countries, there is a shortage of food or people don’t have adequate access to food. In many other countries, people have too much to eat and you have the related problems of obesity and other diseases.\nInterviewer: Is there an optimum, an ideal food?\nProf Catherine Geissler: There’s no one ideal food. In any diet we need a mixture of foods to provide us with all the nutrients that we require. Of course, in every country there’s what we call a staple which is the main source of food.\nInterviewer: So, it’s about a balance then. Is it?\nProf Catherine Geissler: It’s really about a balance between the different types of foods that we have such as cereals and root crops, and then we need the other types of fruits and vegetables which are leafy vegetables, or other types of green and yellow vegetables. And we don’t need large amounts of meat. In fact, we don’t need any meat at all necessarily because the nutrients from meat can be provided elsewhere, but the main thing is to try to have a variety of different types of foods and then you’re much more likely to have all the nutrients that are needed.\nInterviewer: Do you have to balance it out as carefully as that at each meal?\nProf Catherine Geissler: It doesn’t have to be at every meal. Not even within one day do you have to have it balanced but over a few days, if you make sure that you have a very large variety of foods.\nInterviewer: Why might this be, that children, some children then don’t seem to thrive on the food they eat?\nProf Catherine Geissler: I think this depends on the age of the child. If it’s a very young child, sometimes they don’t have the enzymes to actually digest the food, so for example an infant might not be able to digest fat or certain milk sugars when they’re very young, but this shouldn’t be a problem later on in childhood.\nInterviewer: I’ve heard that drinking red wine or even red grape juice can protect you against cancer. Is it true?\nProf Catherine Geissler: There’s been a lot of interest recently in red wine, mainly in relation to heart disease because it’s been found that in countries where you have quite a high-fat intake, but there’s also a large consumption of red wine…\nInterviewer: Are you thinking of France?\nProf Catherine Geissler: I’m thinking of France, that the extent of heart disease is not nearly as high as you would expect.\nInterviewer: Are there any other foods that might protect you from cancer?\nProf Catherine Geissler: The main foods that are protective against cancer are fruits and vegetables in general. We can’t really say there’s one particular fruit or vegetable that would be particularly beneficial, but a mixture of fruit and vegetables in the diet certainly does reduce the cancer risk.\nInterviewer: So, how bad is coffee?\nProf Catherine Geissler: There’s a lot of controversy about coffee and we all know that too much coffee tends to make us nervous but on the other hand, it has good effects such as it makes us more alert. It tends to improve our mood when we’re a bit dejected and down."
