   "transcript": "Presenter: Hello, welcome to the programme. We’re talking about sleep today. Have you had trouble with sleep and what did you do about it? We have one of the UK’s leading sleep experts here to try to help. Dr Neil Stanley was director of sleep research at the University of Surrey and he’s written a book, How to Sleep Well. Dr Stanley, welcome to Call You and Yours.\n[Example]\nDr Neil Stanley: Well I mean what’s funny is if you ask a good sleeper how they sleep well, they look at you like you’ve gone mad because they say you lie down, you close your eyes and you fall asleep, that’s it. So really sleeping, getting to sleep should be like falling off a log. It should be that easy. The problem is we have made it difficult.\n[Question 1]\nDr Neil Stanley: There aren’t 12 tips for getting a good night’s sleep. On my Twitter feed, I’ve today published my 2,290^(th) sleep tip. There’s a myriad of sleep tips.\n[Question 2]\nDr Neil Stanley: The first thing you need to do is see that sleep’s important. You know we now have a way of foregoing sleep. You know, we work long hours, we have a long commute and then we come home and we binge-watch something on a streaming service. If you do that, then you’re not going to get to sleep. If you need eight hours’ sleep and you’re not in bed for eight hours, you’re not going to get eight hours’ sleep. It’s as simple as that. You need a relaxed body. You need to have done something during the day in order to earn sleep at night so exercise, get out in the fresh air and in the sunlight but the most important one, and one that your callers all alluded to, was you need to have a quiet mind, and you cannot fall asleep unless you’ve got a quiet mind.\n[Question 3]\nDr Neil Stanley: I read every night. Whatever time I get home, I read. That’s what I do before bed but I wouldn’t recommend that to everybody.\n[Question 4]\nDr Neil Stanley: Well this is it. I mean, this is a question you need to ask yourself, how do you feel during the day? The big myth in sleep is that we all need eight hours. Eight hours is merely an average. It’s not an ideal. Sleep need is like height. It’s genetically determined and most of us are, you know, individual differences so some people only need four hours’ sleep and they feel absolutely fine. Other people need to have eleven hours’ sleep in order to feel fine during the day so the question now is not how much sleep I had or how well did I sleep, but how do I feel during the day. If you feel awake, alert, focused on your job, you’ve had enough sleep. If you feel sleepy during the day, however much sleep you had wasn’t enough. So there is no magic amount of hours. It’s exactly how you feel during the day.\n[Question 5]\nDr Neil Stanley: Well the sun is the primary signal for our circadian rhythm. It’s known as the Zeitgäber, the time-giver, and it just only takes four minutes of sunshine to tell your body that it’s daytime and then it has to be awake.\n[Question 6]\nDr Neil Stanley: Well I mean shift work is, of course, very very devastating towards sleep. I mean essentially the problem is you’re sleeping during the day when it’s light and therefore the body thinks it’s daytime, and so somebody who’s working nights will sleep for two and a half hours less by sleeping during the day than they would have at night.\n[Question 7]\nDr Neil Stanley: Absolutely and so a solution needs to be found. You know medication does play a role in helping people get to sleep. Cognitive behavioural therapy is improving to help some people fall asleep.\n[Question 8]\nDr Neil Stanley: We do. We need sleep more now than we’ve ever done before because sleep is about processing new information, laying down memories and we are bombarded with information like we’ve never been before. So actually, it’s not that we’re sleeping less. It’s that we actually need the sleep to be able to cope, and the feeling of being overwhelmed, the feeling of stress and anxiety is because we can’t cope with this deluge of information and until we work out our relationship with our mobile phones and our tablets and our PCs, and respect them and actually put them away, I mean you know, when I was growing up, nobody rang the house after 9 o’clock on the landline.\nPresenter: That’s true, yes.\nDr Neil Stanley: So until we – and you know, TV went off at 10.30, and until we go back to that sort of relationship, having a mature relationship with technology, we are going to suffer because our brains just cannot cope and they were never designed to cope with this deluge of information, which means sleep is more important now than it’s ever been.\nPresenter: Dr Neil Stanley, thank you so much for being our expert guest today, and thank you all for listening."
