   "transcript": "Voice-over: We’re going back to Hong Kong in 1967 when for much of the year, the whole territory was engulfed in street protests, sabotage and even bomb attacks. Left-wingers inspired by the Cultural Revolution in mainland China had started demanding an end to British colonial rule and clashes with the police had become increasingly violent. In 2014, Rebecca Kesby spoke to Jasper Tsang Yok-sing about that time.\nThe protests began with a number of small-scale strikes by factory workers calling for better pay. Back then, Jasper Tsang Yok-sing was a student and remembers the desperate conditions for workers in Hong Kong in the factories and at home.\nJasper Tsang Yok-sing: During the 1960s, there were (was) very little protection for workers in factories. We did not have any labour laws. We now have. On the whole, factory workers were quite poorly paid. There were still a lot of families living in what we called squatter huts, very poorly built structures, always threatened by fires or storm, no electricity, no drinking water. Even those who were a little bit better off, like my family who lived in a small room, simply partitioned by pieces of cloth and my whole family, all five of us, were packed within one room but we were still better than many other families actually. My father was working as a junior clerk and he had to do two jobs to earn a living for the family.\nVoice-over: Jasper Tsang Yok-sing was the oldest of three children and managed to get a place at Hong Kong University to study Maths but, as the protests intensified, he felt more compelled to join them.\nJasper Tsang Yok-sing: So, I used to watch evening news with other students and we talked about it. We would have very heated debates. On a couple of occasions I was in the streets when the protests took place and on an impulse I actually joined them because I think it is natural for me to sympathise with the workers who as far as I could see were simply asking for fairer treatment from the employers.\nVoice-over: By the middle of the year, a small militant group of activists had started planting bombs and decoy fake bombs, hundreds of them. They caused widespread disruption, casualties and fear.\nYou talk about the influence of the mainland but in fact it was the Chinese premier, Zhou Enlai, who ordered a halt to the leftist protesters in the end. Did some people feel let down by the Chinese?\nJasper Tsang Yok-sing: Yes, at least some of the protesters believed the Chinese army were coming to liberate Hong Kong from the British, so when Zhou Enlai gave everybody a very clear message that no, no more violence, I believe many of the protesters were disappointed.\nVoice-over: Zhou Enlai’s instruction to end the protest was an indication that China was prepared to play the long game in Hong Kong. Beijing relied on foreign trade through the territory and wasn’t ready to change the status quo, but Jasper Tsang Yok-sing sees the leftist riots of 1967 as a watershed moment for Hong Kong and in his own life."
