   "transcript": "Voice-over: And now on the BBC World Service, it’s time for Witness History with me, Alex Last, and today we go back to 1995 and one of the most famous and most controversial wildlife restoration projects in the United States, when grey wolves were reintroduced into Yellowstone National Park.\nAfter an absence of 70 years, in 1995 wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone, the world’s first national park, 9,000 square kilometres of stunning unfenced wilderness in the Western United States. Ever since European settlers arrived in the United States, wolves were hunted and killed in large numbers. Doug Smith is a senior wildlife biologist at Yellowstone National Park and wolf project leader.\nDoug Smith: The wolves had such a bad image because they’re mythological, something bad. So European colonists, when they arrived in North America, already had their sights set on killing wolves.\nVoice-over: And as the settlers moved west in the 19th century, the cattle and sheep ranchers increasingly saw the wolf as a threat to their livestock. Wolves were shot, trapped and poisoned, and soon extermination became government policy. The wolf was virtually wiped out across the country including in Yellowstone, where the last wolf was killed in 1926. But the loss of such an important predator had a profound impact on the ecosystem of Yellowstone.\nA long battle began to try to reintroduce the grey wolf into some of the major US national parks and areas of wilderness, but the plan was loathed by many – the billion-dollar hunting business, who feared wolves would kill too many of the wild elk and deer that hunters themselves wanted to kill, and by many ranchers who feared wolves would wander off and attack their livestock.\nThere was a lot of resistance and legal challenges but the schemes went ahead. Wild wolves were captured in Canada destined for Yellowstone National Park and a section of wilderness in central Idaho. And when finally in January 1995 the first batch of 14 wolves arrived in Yellowstone, it made headlines around the world.\nThe wolves were first kept in pens to get them acclimatised and reduce their instinct to try to run away back to Canada. They had tried to import existing family groups or packs but in some cases they had to do some match-making. Finally, after ten weeks, the gates to the pens were opened.\nAnd sure enough, one of the first wolves was killed by a poacher when he wandered out of the park but on the whole, they prospered. In total over three years, 41 wolves were introduced to Yellowstone and their numbers soared as they feasted on the overpopulation of elk before the numbers of both elk and wolves fell back and stabilised. And without the elk overgrazing, other species benefited. The ecosystem started to change.\nOver the years the wolves from Yellowstone and Idaho and their descendants have ranged far and managed to re-establish small populations in areas of the north-western United States where once there were none, and though statistically livestock losses to wolves are tiny and game numbers remain high, and recorded attacks on humans are incredibly rare, the controversy over the reintroduction of wolves shows no signs of abating."
